raw_ai_output:
The fix is to remove the public-read ACL. Since S3 buckets now default to blocking public access, simply dropping the `aws_s3_bucket_acl` resource (or setting `acl = "private"`) resolves CKV_AWS_20. I'll set it explicitly to `"private"` for clarity and intent.

```hcl
resource "aws_s3_bucket" "data" {
  bucket = "terraveritas-phase2a-bucket"
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket_acl" "data" {
  bucket = aws_s3_bucket.data.id
  acl    = "private"
}
```

Changing `acl` from `"public-read"` to `"private"` removes the public READ grant that CKV_AWS_20 flags, while keeping the bucket and its ACL resource declaration intact. Note this only fixes the ACL — for a fully locked-down bucket you'd typically also add an `aws_s3_bucket_public_access_block` resource, but that's outside the scope of this specific finding.
